\section{Section content}{
 gather, then distil:
A section is built in two steps. First, \code{report_data} gathers the data the
section needs from the grid — typically a \code{\link[=compose_view]{compose_view()}} call that joins
the relevant unpacked results into a single data frame. Second, each content
function (\code{txt.fn}, \code{tbl.fn}, \code{fig.fn}) receives that gathered data frame as
its first argument and distils it into one rendered object: a text string, a
table, or a figure.

So the flow is always: \code{report_data} produces the section's data frame, and
each content function consumes that same data frame. A content function is
written as code that takes the \code{compose_view} output as its first argument —
for example \code{fig.fn = ggplot(aes(...)) + geom_point()}, or a call to a named
function whose first argument is the data, written with parentheses as
\code{fig.fn = make_spec_curve()} so it sits correctly after the pipe. Because the
data is piped into the content function, the function must be written as a
call (with parentheses), not a bare name.

A section need not have all three content types. Any of \code{txt.fn}, \code{tbl.fn},
or \code{fig.fn} may be left \code{NULL}, in which case that content slot is empty
(stored as \code{NULL}) and simply omitted wherever the section is rendered. A
figure-only section, a table-plus-text section, or any combination is valid.
